    "The most defensible story is simple: the passage appears to continue an argument already in progress. One line may preserve the end of a phrase meaning “of the things said”; another may contain a relational word such as “toward”, “against”, or “with regard to”; the strongest grammatical pattern is a repeated negative, roughly “neither ... nor ...”. That gives the fragment a coherent argumentative shape even though the middle words are broken.",
    "Within the Herculaneum library context, this kind of phrasing fits philosophical prose better than poetry, narrative, or a document. It could belong to an Epicurean discussion, but the segment does not securely identify an author or title. The honest conclusion is a plausible reading path rather than a claimed translation.",
    "For presentation, the important result is the workflow: CT-derived ink evidence is turned into candidate letters, multiple model readings are compared, and the interface separates visible evidence from interpretation."
